## Review.cpp — Algorithmic Problem

**What it does:** Counts how many elements from `keys` also appear in `data`.

**The problem:** A **nested loop** — for each key, it scans the entire `data` vector.

**Complexity:** **O(n × m)** — quadratic in the worst case, where `n = keys.size()` and `m = data.size()`.

**The fix:** Replace the inner linear scan with a **`std::unordered_set<int>`** built from `data`. Lookup becomes O(1) amortized per key, dropping total complexity to **O(n + m)**.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_set>

int lookup(const std::vector<int>& keys, const std::vector<int>& data) {
  std::unordered_set<int> s(data.begin(), data.end());
  int hits = 0;
  for (int k : keys)
    if (s.count(k)) ++hits;
  return hits;
}
```

**Summary:** Quadratic nested-loop lookup → O(n+m) with a hash set.

## Performance Problem in `Review.cpp`

**Line 5:** `for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;`

### Cause: Avoidable reallocations from repeated appends

Each `out += p` may trigger a reallocation of `std::string`'s internal buffer when the current capacity is exceeded. In the worst case this means O(n) reallocations, each copying the entire accumulated string so far — turning an O(total_length) operation into O(total_length²).

### Idiomatic fix: `reserve` when the total size is known

Compute the total length upfront and call `out.reserve(total)` before the loop, so the string allocates exactly once:

```cpp
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts) {
  std::string out;
  for (const auto& p : parts) out.reserve(out.size() + p.size());  // or pre-sum
  for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;
  return out;
}
```

Or more cleanly, sum first:

```cpp
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts) {
  std::string out;
  for (const auto& p : parts) out.reserve(out.size() + p.size());
  for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;
  return out;
}
```

The `reserve` call eliminates all intermediate reallocations — the string grows in-place with a single allocation.

